¶
'''bread'''<sup>1</sup> (bred), ''v. t.'' [< ''bread''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] In ''cookery'', to prepare with grated bread; cover with white of eggs and bread-crumbs.

'''bread'''<sup>2</sup> (brēd), ''v. t.'' [< ME. ''breden'', < AS. ''brǣdan'' (= OS. ''brēdian'' = OHG. ''breitēn'', MHG. G. ''breiten'' = Icel. ''breidhja'' = Sw. ''breda'' = Dan. ''brede'' = Goth. *''braidjan'', in comp. ''us-braidjan''), make broad, < ''brād'', broad: see ''broad'', ''a.'', and cf. ''broad'', ''v.'', and ''broaden''.] To make broad; spread. ''Ray''; ''Grose''. [Prov. Eng.]

'''bread'''<sup>2</sup>†, ''n.'' [< ME. ''brede'', < AS. ''brǣdu'' (= D. ''breedte'' = OHG. ''breitī'', MHG. G. ''breite'' = Icel. ''breidd'' = Sw. ''bredd'' = Dan. ''bredde'' = Goth. ''braidei''), breadth, < ''brād'', broad: see ''broad''.] Breadth. Also ''brede''. [The older word, now displaced by ''breadth''.]
{{smaller block|Thoughe it be clept the Tour of Babiloyne, ȝit natheles there were ordeyned with inne many Mansiouns and many gret duellynge Places, in length and ''brede''.
{{right|''Mandeville'', Travels, p. 41.}}}}
'''On brede'''†, {{smaller|abroad.}}
{{smaller block|Sorwe yblowe ''on brede''. ''Chaucer'', Troilus, i. 530.}}

'''bread'''<sup>3</sup> (brēd), ''v. t.'' [Var. of ''braid''<sup>1</sup>; < ME. ''breden'', < AS. ''bredan'', ''bregdan'': see ''braid''<sup>1</sup>.] In ''net-making'', to form in meshes; net. Also ''breathe'', ''brede''.
{{smaller block|To ''bread'' or ''breathe'' a net is to make a net.
{{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', XVII. 359.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
¶
'''bread'''<sup>2</sup>, '''brede''' (bred), ''n.'' [Var. of ''braid''<sup>1</sup>, ''n.''] A piece of embroidery; a braid. [Obsolete or poetical.]
{{smaller block|A curious ''brede'' of needlework. {{right|''Dryden.''}}
She every day came to him in a different dress, of the most beautiful shells, bugles, and ''bredes.''
{{right|''Steele'', Spectator, No. 11.}}
<poem>O Attic shape! Fair attitude! with ''brede''
Of marble men and maidens overwrought.</poem>
{{right|''Keats'', Ode on a Grecian Urn.}}
<poem>The . . . wave that rims the Carib shore
With momentary ''brede'' of pearl and gold.</poem>
{{right|''Lowell'', Sea-weed.}}}}

'''bread-and-butter''' (bred′a̤nd-but′ėr), ''a.'' '''1.''' Seeking bread and butter, or the means of living; controlled by material wants and desires; mercenary: as, the ''bread-and-butter'' brigade (applied to office-seekers in the United States). —'''2.''' Eating much bread and butter, as young boys or girls; hence, belonging to adolescence; in the stage of growth: as, she’s but a ''bread-and-butter'' miss. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|The wishy-washy ''bread-and-butter'' period of life.
{{right|''Trollope'', Barchester Towers, xli.}}}}

'''bread-barge''' (bred′bärj), ''n.'' The wooden box or tub in which the crew of a merchant vessel keep their daily allowance of biscuit.

'''bread-basket''' (bred′bȧs″ket), ''n.'' '''1.''' A basket for holding or carrying bread; specifically, a tray, generally oval in shape, used for holding bread at table.—'''2.''' The stomach. [Slang.]
{{smaller block|I . . . made the soup-maigre rumble in his ''bread-basket'', and laid him sprawling. {{right|''Foote'', Englishman in Paris, i.}}}}

